# The commits the tour applies, each in its own named graph.
#
# A delta is not RDF that goes anywhere — it is the *argument* to a tick. Keeping the four of
# them in one file, one graph each, means the tour can name a commit and you can read exactly
# what it proposes without running anything.
#
# They are ordered so that each one shows a different way a boundary can answer.

@prefix wo:   <https://example.org/workorders#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix d:    <https://example.org/deltas#> .

# 1. An ordinary, conforming commit: raise a new order. Accepted.
d:raise-1002 {
    wo:WO-1002
        a wo:WorkOrder ;
        wo:asset wo:compressor-3 ;
        wo:status wo:Raised ;
        wo:raisedOn "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
        wo:summary "Annual service due." .
}

# 2. A commit the boundary refuses: a status the vocabulary does not have. Everything about
#    this order is otherwise fine, which is the point — the tick is refused whole, and the
#    scene does not end up holding the good half.
d:raise-1003-bad {
    wo:WO-1003
        a wo:WorkOrder ;
        wo:asset wo:pump-27 ;
        wo:status wo:Awaiting-Parts ;
        wo:raisedOn "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
        wo:summary "Bearing noise on start-up." .
}

# 3. A commit that fires a rule, and the only one here that takes something away.
#
#    A delta is a pair of sets, and the tour reads the removals from a graph named after the
#    addition graph. Moving the status from InProgress to Closed means both: RDF has no
#    notion of updating a value in place, so the old statement has to go or the order ends up
#    holding two statuses and failing its own `sh:maxCount 1`.
#
#    The sign-off is what makes the boundary's rule fire, deriving `wo:ClosedOrder`; the
#    completion date is what that classification then requires. Both halves are here, so the
#    commit is accepted.
d:signoff-1001 {
    wo:WO-1001
        wo:signedOffBy wo:eng-mahmoud ;
        wo:completedOn "2026-09-03"^^xsd:date ;
        wo:status wo:Closed .
}

d:signoff-1001-remove {
    wo:WO-1001 wo:status wo:InProgress .
}

# 4. A commit where the rule is what causes the refusal. The same sign-off, on the order
#    raised in commit 1, but with no completion date. The rule derives `wo:ClosedOrder`; the
#    boundary then sees a closed order with nothing recording when it was completed, and
#    refuses the whole commit.
#
#    This is the case that shows why rules run *before* validation rather than after. Run
#    afterwards, this would already have been written when it was found to be wrong.
d:signoff-1002-incomplete {
    wo:WO-1002 wo:signedOffBy wo:eng-mahmoud .
}
